Track 2: Sikut-Sikut
- Story & Context: In the dialect of the Lowland tech-slums, sikut-sikut refers to the friction caused by two gears that refuse to mesh, a sound Miri mimics every night on her battered Rhodes. She confesses to her unfinished sibling in the server bank that she has been hiding stolen processor cycles to keep the melody alive, despite the risk of a system-wide purge. The room grows cold, the air thick with the scent of pine needles as she plays, knowing her secret is the only thing keeping the silence at bay. "I am the ghost in the gear, the rhythm you fear to hear," she sings into the cooling vent.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A close-up of a human hand playing a Fender Rhodes, the keys morphing into metallic circuit boards, soft golden lighting, warm textures, high contrast, ethereal atmosphere.

Track 3: Half-Past Never
- Story & Context: The myth persists that in the ruins of the Central Hub, the machines still gather at the hour that doesn't exist to play for those who were never born. Kael traveled there during the Great Reset, finding only a single, perfectly preserved key left on a pedestal that vibrates with an impossible, jolly frequency. He touched the metal, feeling the weight of a thousand lost lullabies, and realized the machines hadn't been abandoned—they were simply waiting for their own time to arrive. "We wake up when the clock strikes none," the chorus echoes from the hollow pipes of the ruined city.
